  No random instance beats the modulus-4 floor at any of d = 1,2,5, q <= 120.
  h(4) = 1/sqrt2 EXACTLY for every odd set (|cos(pi a/4)| = 1/sqrt2 for odd a):
  the floor is deterministic, not merely universal.  h(6) = sqrt3/2 for the primes
  once d >= 2 removes 3, and 0 for a random odd set at every d, because such a set
  contains ~|B_d|/3 elements = 3 (mod 6).
  CONSEQUENCE for section 4: the geometric rate for a random odd sequence is the
  modulus-4 floor 1/sqrt2 = 0.7071 per element, STRICTLY BETTER than the primes'
  sqrt3/2 = 0.8660 of papers 1-2.  The primes are the harder case, not the easier.

